.TH iron-vault-add 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-add \- Enrol a new credential under NAME; the service prompts for the secret, read with echo off or from stdin, so `pass show fx | iron\-vault add fx \-\-origin api.fx.example` works. It never overwrites an existing name
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault add\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-kind\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fB\-\-origin\fR> <\fINAME\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Enrol a new credential under NAME; the service prompts for the secret, read with echo off or from stdin, so `pass show fx | iron\-vault add fx \-\-origin api.fx.example` works. It never overwrites an existing name
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fI<KIND>\fR [default: static]
The credential\*(Aqs kind: a static key, or a Basic username and password
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
static: A provider key, sent as is
.IP \(bu 2
basic: A username and password, sent as `Authorization: Basic`
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-origin\fR \fI<ORIGIN>\fR
A permitted origin: `host`, `host:port`, `[v6]` or `[v6]:port`, without a scheme (HTTPS is implied); the port defaults to 443. Repeat for each origin, up to 32
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
The credential name, which routes reference
